<!--
    GH-8724: a compiler-lowered `sizeof(*p)*i` array offset is not recovered as
    an index.  https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/8724

    Source (essence):  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) use( base[i].field[ base[i].k ] );
                       where sizeof(base[i]) == 0x414.
    Compiled (x86-64): the compiler strength-reduces the array walk into a
                       loop-carried OFFSET accumulator that starts at 0 and is
                       bumped by the element size (0x414) every iteration, in
                       lock-step with the unit-step loop counter:
                           3d0: xor %ebp,%ebp        ; offset acc = 0
                           3d3: xor %ebx,%ebx        ; counter   = 0
                           3e4: add $0x1,%rbx        ; counter += 1
                           3eb: add $0x414,%rbp      ; acc     += 0x414  (stride)
                       The original multiply `i * 0x414` never appears in the
                       machine code, so Ghidra's pointer/PTRADD recovery has
                       nothing to latch onto and the index `i` stays invisible.

    Default output:    the raw strided accumulator survives into the rendered C
                       (`iVar3 = iVar3 + 0x414;`), so the `+ 0x414` token appears.

    Stage model: this is the S3 "simplification-quiescence" sub-stage.  In SSA the
    two sibling loop-header phis
        acc = MULTIEQUAL(#0, acc + 0x414)     // strided offset accumulator
        cnt = MULTIEQUAL(#0, cnt + 1)         // unit-step counter
    share predecessor edges and both start at 0, so `acc == cnt * 0x414` exactly
    (the algebraic reverse of induction-variable strength reduction).  The P0
    assertion `option arraystride on|off` (default off = upstream byte-identical)
    gates RuleArrayStride, which rewrites the accumulator as `cnt * 0x414`,
    re-exposing the index `i` (`* 0x414` token) and dropping the accumulator.

    The script decompiles the function twice: pass 1 under the default pipeline
    (assert #1 proves the issue reproduces: the `+ 0x414` accumulator), pass 2
    with `option arraystride on` (assert #2 proves the stage-level intervention
    recovers the index: the `* 0x414` multiply).  Each token occurs exactly once
    across the combined stream, so each form must appear exactly once.
-->
